window.BOARD = {"rendered":"2026-11-26T08:19:25Z","prime_owner":"neve","types":["sketch","bug","enhancement","feature","research"],"areas":["board","config","ide","reed","fabric","selfreport","shuttle","burler","webster","stencil","loom","quarry","batten","shed","orch","creel","claude","codex","report","workspaces","deploy","dotnet","gate","testkit","pattern","shortcut","dobby","tender","hibernate","drift","darn","arbiter","landing","cleanup","termwindow","logger","git","cwd","friction","lenses","reconcile","preflight","parsers","process","fsutil","cli","pawl","lint","profiles","docs","heddle","tools","slack","weaver","testmap"],"sections":["Running","Layer A","Research","Bugs","Enhancements","Features"],"projects":[{"handle":"P0","slug":"root","title":"Root: entries under no project","brief":"","labels":[],"parent":"","design_file":"","done":false},{"handle":"P1","slug":"intake-wave-1","title":"Intake wave 1: PR modes, Webster, dotnet gate, reed, creel, commits","brief":"","labels":["enhancement"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"","done":false},{"handle":"P2","slug":"borrowed-orchestrator-ideas","title":"Ideas borrowed from comparable agent orchestrators","brief":"","labels":["feature","burler","shed","shuttle","orch","report"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"design-borrowed-orchestrator-ideas.md","done":false},{"handle":"P3","slug":"intake-wave-2","title":"Intake wave 2: Describe text, infra stops, push retries","brief":"","labels":["enhancement"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"","done":false},{"handle":"P4","slug":"single-repo-hub","title":"Single-repository hubs: records and board under refs/lym/, the layout known only to fabric","brief":"","labels":["feature","fabric","cwd","cli","landing","docs","pattern","gate","git","weaver","drift","webster","lint","loom","pawl","shed","arbiter"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"design-single-repo-hub.md","done":false},{"handle":"P5","slug":"intake-wave-3","title":"Intake wave 3: beam orch parent, scope and earlier checks","brief":"","labels":["enhancement"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"","done":false},{"handle":"P6","slug":"intake-motifs","title":"Intake motifs: run watch, landed records, closed area","brief":"","labels":["enhancement"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"","done":false}],"entries":[{"handle":"T8","slug":"remove-record-rerun-pair-gone","title":"A killed batten cancel leaves a remove record whose way forward fails once the pair is gone","kind":"task","types":["bug"],"areas":[],"status":"running · Darn-Write","project":"P0","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"The record names lym fabric remove --remote, which answers pair not found; batten cancel --remove is the re-run that works","body":"## Seen\n\nLive drive of the one-repository sandbox (`docs/research/single-repo-undriven-cases.md`).\n`lym batten cancel \u003cslug\u003e --remove`, killed while its detached `lym fabric remote-teardown` child stalled on an origin branch delete,\nleaves a pending record of kind `remove`.\n`lym fabric status` lists it with the way forward `... run \\`lym fabric remove \u003cslug\u003e --remote\\` again`.\nRun once the pair was gone, that command answers `{\"error\":\"pair not found: nothing of \\\"disc1\\\" remains\",\"ok\":false}`.\nThe command that finished the teardown was `lym batten cancel \u003cslug\u003e --remove`.\n\n## Idea\n\nWhen the record's pair is gone, or the record came from a batten cancel, name `lym batten cancel \u003cslug\u003e --remove` as the re-run,\nthe way the `discard` record's `rerun` field already does.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-23T13:57:29Z","revised":"2026-11-23T14:27:27Z","design_file":"design-remove-record-rerun-pair-gone.md","decision_file":"","revision":"550ad8d53d1221a1188e5730dc4759af5931b5fc82c780a18b8a71f859c428b3"},{"handle":"T3","slug":"config-keys-nested","title":"Nest loom and tapestry config keys by area, move old names on reconcile, and name every key in full","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["config","loom","reconcile","profiles","shed","batten","fabric","drift","landing","gate","webster","shuttle","dobby","claude","codex","stencil","pattern","docs"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Layer A","waits_on":["T8"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `config-keys-nested-by-area`.\n\nTakes: the Sketch's decision record; the flat loom and tapestry templates and their readers; configengine's dotted-path layering, configsync's reconcile, hubreconcile's walk and EnsureOrWarn, configreg.PrepareRunSettings and boardengine's upsert path\nDelivers: loom.yaml and tapestry.yaml nested one map per area with every reader on the nested paths, pr_review_parent_timeout_min (read by autonomy mode) included as pr.review_parent_timeout_min; a move table that every reconcile, the profiles follow and each hub-wide walk apply to file layers, profiles.yaml blocks and board run_settings; refusal of an old name at every site with that site's way forward; EnsureOrWarn before every non-config verb that loads loom or tapestry config; every key named in full in Go messages, template comments, contracts, pattern and docs outside docs/research; PATTERN-config-layers changed and PATTERN-config-key-names added\nExports: configengine.KeyName, configengine.Spec.MovedKeys, the configreg helper that moves a module-keyed settings object, the helper that nests a dotted path into a run_settings or profile object\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/loomengine (about 450 changed lines)\n- internal/reviewbudget (about 120 changed lines)\n- internal/configengine (about 300 changed lines)\n- internal/configsync (about 350 changed lines)\n- internal/hubreconcile (about 300 changed lines)\n- internal/configreg (about 250 changed lines)\n- internal/configcli (about 40 changed lines)\n- internal/runprofile (about 120 changed lines)\n- internal/beamcli (about 50 changed lines)\n- internal/shedrecipe (about 100 changed lines)\n- internal/loomrecipe (about 80 changed lines)\n- internal/loomcli (about 300 changed lines)\n- internal/loomshed (about 20 changed lines)\n- internal/shedadapters (about 60 changed lines)\n- internal/battencli (about 80 changed lines)\n- internal/hardenshed (about 20 changed lines)\n- internal/fabricengine (about 40 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriccli (about 40 changed lines)\n- internal/driftengine (about 40 changed lines)\n- internal/landingshed (about 30 changed lines)\n- internal/gateslot (about 30 changed lines)\n- internal/webstercli (about 20 changed lines)\n- internal/shuttleparity (about 20 changed lines)\n- internal/preflightshed (about 10 changed lines)\n- internal/dobbyengine (about 20 changed lines)\n- internal/claudeengine (about 10 changed lines)\n- internal/codexengine (about 10 changed lines)\n- contracts (about 200 changed lines)\n- docs (about 80 changed lines)\n- pattern (about 60 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-23T13:25:15Z","revised":"2026-11-23T19:58:05Z","design_file":"design-config-keys-nested.md","decision_file":"decision-config-keys-nested.md","revision":"a75b9b7b4fa1937a558c47790d07221f7a3e3dab4b541bddfbb5b776011913fb"},{"handle":"B1","slug":"pr-ask-live-pane-test","title":"No tmux-tier test sends parent-review ask from a live non-orch pane","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["landing","creel"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Seen\n\n`failed-ask-hides-satisfied` (issue 213) left out its item 3: no test runs `lym loom parent-review ask` from a live non-orch reed strand pane.\nThat is the path that failed twice on 2026-11-23 in the mo hub (creel refused the LOOM signature), and the fix (708cdadc5) is pinned only below the tmux tier.\n\n## Idea\n\nA tmux-tier test: a built lym binary in a reed strand pane, a hub with a pull request record under autonomy, and a live orch strand that receives the post; assert the ask is delivered and the parent's answer lets `pr-continue` end the review.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-23T18:56:36Z","revised":"2026-11-23T18:56:36Z","design_file":"design-pr-ask-live-pane-test.md","decision_file":"","revision":"80c2f5cb860f3c5c800d26eb511a1c9b61f25f74927d605820623881b281ee0a"},{"handle":"B2","slug":"reed-switch-wrong-client","title":"reed switch and a button's {strand} read the most recent client's session, not the clicker's","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":[],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"Same display-message -c with no -t defect the RC click had, in switch.go and buttonclick.go","body":"`switchClientVia` (`internal/reedengine/switch.go`) reads the clicking client's `#{session_id}` and `runButtonVia`'s strand lookup (`internal/reedengine/buttonclick.go`) reads `#{@lym_strand_name}`, each with `display-message -p -c \u003cclient\u003e` and no `-t`.\nRun from outside tmux, tmux expands that format against the most recently active client's session, so on a server with clients in several sessions `reed switch` can step from another client's session and a `{strand}` button can name another session's agent.\nThe RC click had the same defect and now reads its row of `list-clients -F`, which tmux expands per client; `TestClientAgentPane_ReadsEachClientsOwnSession` shows the two-client setup a test of these needs.","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-23T20:51:38Z","revised":"2026-11-23T20:51:38Z","design_file":"design-reed-switch-wrong-client.md","decision_file":"","revision":"12eda1a42d97ec7deb13e78e54f371d2af9fe580b93d31aa16f4718eea4a521c"},{"handle":"B3","slug":"revive-reads-placeholder-draft","title":"tender revive reads Claude Code placeholder after an API error as a typed draft and skips the session","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["tender","claude"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Seen\n\nOn 2026-11-26 two Claude sessions stalled on `API Error: Connection refused (ECONNREFUSED)`: `ly:rc-click-wrong-client-session:darn` and `ly:async-run-pusher:code-burler-fix`.\n`lym tender revive` skipped both with reason `the input box holds a draft: \"Resend your message to try again\"`.\nThat text is Claude Code's own prompt after an API error, not text anyone typed, so revive never reaches the case it exists for.\nNo code on `origin/main` names the string.\n\n## Workaround\n\nThe orch paused each run with `lym batten pause \u003cslug\u003e` and resumed it with `lym batten run \u003cslug\u003e`, which relaunches the row's agent on its own session.\n\n## Expected\n\nThe input-box reader in `internal/shuttleengine/claudeengine` treats Claude Code's post-error prompt as an empty box, and `tender revive` gives the session its carry-on line.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:25:04Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:25:04Z","design_file":"design-revive-reads-placeholder-draft.md","decision_file":"","revision":"c956a232ec13f81d0391502a09422648f96308b7d6958906b83eb0b171bac6db"},{"handle":"B4","slug":"gate-fixer-reboot-reads-timeout","title":"Gate fixer interrupted by a machine stop is reported as dead (timeout)","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["gate","shuttle"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #248](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/248).\n\n## What happened\n\nA row gate spawned a cold fixer, and the machine rebooted under a minute later.\nThe run was re-stepped more than two days after that.\nThe loop's reconcile had stopped the fixer's shuttle run, but the gate fixer's record still read live.\nThe restarted driver waited on that record, classified it `dead` with reason `timeout`, and told the writer:\n\n\u003e The cold fixer ended without a reply (timeout); any change it left uncommitted is in the worktree.\n\nThe interrupted-stop detail, from the first entry after the reboot:\n\n```\nloop exit: not recorded, since no process marked the loop's exit, and the machine has booted since the loop started, so a stop of the machine likely ended it; (...) stopped shuttle runs: ly:\u003cslug\u003e:gate-fixer; left runs whose output files all exist: ly:\u003cslug\u003e:darn\n```\n\nThe fixer's shuttle `run.json` (excerpt):\n\n```\n\"createdAt\": \"2026-11-23T19:19:20Z\",\n\"outcome\": \"stopped\",\n\"roleKey\": \"gate-fixer\",\n\"interrupted\": true\n```\n\nThe gate fixer's `records.json` after the resume (excerpt):\n\n```\n\"spawnedAt\": \"2026-11-23T19:19:27Z\",\n\"status\": \"dead\",\n\"deadReason\": \"timeout\"\n```\n\n## Why it is abnormal\n\nThe fixer did not run out of time: the machine stop ended it, and lym had already recorded its run as interrupted.\n`Await` in `internal/gatefixer/await.go` checks the elapsed time since spawn before it checks whether the strand is live,\nso any fixer interrupted by a stop that outlasts its timeout is reported as `timeout` instead of `died`.\nThe writer and anyone reading the record are sent looking for a slow fixer or a timeout that is too short.\n\nThe dead record also stays in the visit's set, which `internal/gatefixer/rowgate.go` counts against the fixer budget.\nThe interrupted fixer's session is not resumed, so the failure it was given falls back to the writer.\n\n## What to do\n\n- Report a fixer whose run is marked interrupted, or whose strand is gone across a reboot, as interrupted or `died`, not `timeout`.\n- Consider resuming its session, or spawning a replacement that does not use up the budget, as is done for interrupted run agents.\n\nProvenance: task `sandbox-without-new-kernel`, note `loom-halt.md`.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:32:58Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:32:58Z","design_file":"design-gate-fixer-reboot-reads-timeout.md","decision_file":"","revision":"907ae3e8dc33c16565ca82d062ed90ae87fd8aaa6f33b76625a584fd4d2a46fb"},{"handle":"B5","slug":"creel-refusal-after-cd","title":"creel post refusal after a persisted cd does not say why","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["creel","cwd"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #245](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/245).\n\nAn agent's survey commands used `cd` into subdirectories, and the shell kept that directory for later calls.\nThe next `lym creel post` was refused because the working directory was no longer the anchored worktree root, which cost a retry.\n\nWhy it is abnormal:\n- The shell's directory persisting across calls is easy to trip over, and the refusal surfaced only on the later creel call, away from the `cd` that caused it.\n\nWhat to do:\n- Have the refusal name the anchored root and say that a previous `cd` likely moved the shell, or accept calls from any directory inside the worktree.\n- Mention in the agent guidance that `cd` persists and absolute paths or `git -C` avoid it.\n\nProvenance: task `retire-d9`; note Darn-Write.md.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:09Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:33:09Z","design_file":"design-creel-refusal-after-cd.md","decision_file":"","revision":"ccabf543c3df97a9d8d7ae3dae380eb150fd89e68bcc396c4e3b75cbb335143a"},{"handle":"B6","slug":"describe-timeout-no-detail","title":"Describe halts failed on an uninformative shuttle run outcome timeout","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["landing","shuttle"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #244](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/244).\n\nThe Describe step halted the run as `failed` with anomaly `producer-hard-failure` and the message `shedadapters: Describe (seats): shuttle run outcome timeout`.\n\nWhy it is abnormal:\n- The note carries no detail on which timeout elapsed, how long the shuttle run had been going, or whether the agent was still working.\n- A timeout on a seated shuttle run is classed as a hard failure, so the run stopped as `failed` rather than being resumed or escalated.\n- The next attempt of the same step completed (`Describe / done`) about 34 minutes later, which suggests the timeout was not a defect in the producer's work.\n\nWhat to do:\n- Include the elapsed time, the timeout limit and the shuttle run's last known state in the halt reason.\n- Decide whether a shuttle outcome timeout should be a retryable or `blocked` stop instead of `failed`.\n\nProvenance: task `retire-d9`; note loom-halt.md.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:13Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:33:13Z","design_file":"design-describe-timeout-no-detail.md","decision_file":"","revision":"f1375f6530e69180244f39818692d56dcb775447a93d3aeeb25d52e45e6eeff0"},{"handle":"B7","slug":"unreachable-origin-halts-failed","title":"Unreachable origin halts the run as failed repeatedly instead of one retryable stop","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["shed","landing","fabric"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #243](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/243).\n\nAn unreachable origin halted the run as `failed` with anomaly `producer-hard-failure` seven times in a row, across PR-Review and Finalize, instead of waiting or blocking once.\n\nWhat happened:\n- PR-Review and Finalize halted with `landingshed: ... push the task branch ... fabricengine: ls-remote of \u003ctask\u003e-weft ... Recv failure: Connection reset by peer`.\n- Finalize then halted three more times with `landingshed: Finalize: read who owns the run: board: origin is unreachable, so the owner write was not pushed: fetching origin for the board ... failed (gitrepo: ... git exited 128 ...)`.\n- One halt in between was `blocked` with anomaly `escalation-to-human`: `owner check: origin was unreachable, so who owns this run could not be judged; re-run \"lym loom resume\" in the task worktree once origin answers`.\n- Each halt wrote a note and a history entry; the history shows `Finalize / stuck` then `Finalize / goto` before each re-failure.\n\nWhy it is abnormal:\n- A transient network failure is classed as a hard failure, the same class as a producer defect, and the run state becomes `failed`.\n- The same condition surfaced under three different halt shapes (`failed`, `blocked`, `failed`), so the operator cannot tell one outage from three problems.\n- Each re-step re-ran immediately and failed again within about a minute, so the retries consumed attempts without waiting for the remote.\n\nWhat to do:\n- Classify unreachable-origin errors (ls-remote, fetch exit 128, connection reset) as one retryable or `blocked` kind with a single note, and have the driver wait or back off before re-stepping.\n- Keep `failed` for errors that retrying cannot fix.\n\nProvenance: task `retire-d9`; notes loom-halt-3.md, loom-halt-4.md, loom-halt-5.md, loom-halt-6.md, loom-halt-7.md, loom-halt-8.md, loom-halt-9.md.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:16Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:33:16Z","design_file":"design-unreachable-origin-halts-failed.md","decision_file":"","revision":"e26d746a873fecd8f9f2492265617996382304dea9b2b1bcb51de4a7ebde57b2"},{"handle":"B8","slug":"finalize-ignored-leftover-dir","title":"Finalize blocks on untracked leftovers of a project the merge-in deleted","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["landing"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #242](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/242).\n\n## Seen\n\nA darn run's Finalize merged its parent beam into the task worktree after its pull request had merged on GitHub.\nThe beam had just taken in a main commit that deleted a whole C# project.\nThe task worktree still held that project's directory with only untracked, gitignored content: `bin/`, `obj/` and generated fixtures.\nOnce git removed the tracked files, the directory was no longer ignored by the deleted `.gitignore`, so it showed as untracked.\nFinalize then blocked: \"the task worktree has uncommitted changes after the merge-in: …/NORCE.Import.CommonLibHarness/; commit or remove them on the task branch, then resume\".\n\nThe run had no live agent left to remove it.\nThe orch may not edit a task worktree, and the Go driver only asked the parent a question and stopped again on resume.\nThe only ways forward were a goto back to the writer, which would re-run review and PR rows for a merged pull request, or the operator removing the directory by hand.\n\n## Workaround\n\nThe operator removes the leftover directory by hand, then the orch runs `lym loom resume` in the task worktree.\n\n## Expected\n\nFinalize's merge-in treats a directory that holds only files the merge's base ignored, under a path the merge deleted, as leftover output:\nit removes it, or ignores it in the clean-tree check, and goes on.\nFailing that, the stop names a lym verb the parent can run to clear it, instead of needing a hand edit in the task worktree.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:20Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:33:20Z","design_file":"design-finalize-ignored-leftover-dir.md","decision_file":"","revision":"940dd59efe6bda35158e686fe57aee4c7c21fc278cd76fe32ddfbd5d52f3e334"},{"handle":"B9","slug":"describe-held-turn-waits-timeout","title":"Describe seat waits out the full timeout after an operator interrupt, then halts as hard failure","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["landing","shuttle"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #241](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/241).\n\nThe Describe producer (seats engine) halted the run as `failed` with `shuttle run outcome timeout`, after its agent session sat held for about 28 minutes with no output.\n\nSequence from the trace:\n\n```\n22:15:05 shuttle: run started role=describe\n22:16:51 shuttle: session state state=idle-stalled cause=interrupt\n22:16:53 shuttle: session state state=busy cause=turn\n22:16:57 shuttle: turn end held, output files missing\n22:16:57 shuttle: held turn end of a turn the operator started; no hold notice\n22:16:57 shuttle: session state state=idle-stalled cause=no-output loop=held\n22:45:06 shuttle: run finished outcome=timeout cleanedUp=false\n22:45:29 shed: step refused kind=producer transient=\"\" error=\"shedadapters: Describe (seats): shuttle run outcome timeout\"\n```\n\nThe halt note reads `loom halted failed at Describe`, `anomaly: producer-hard-failure`, 12 history entries.\n\nWhy this is abnormal:\n- The operator interrupted the agent once and typed into the pane, which made the turn \"operator-started\".\n  lym then held the turn end without a hold notice and waited silently until the run timeout.\n- The session was `idle-stalled` for the whole wait, and nothing re-prompted the agent for its missing output files or surfaced the state to the operator.\n- The timeout was then recorded as a hard failure with `transient=\"\"`, although the cause was an interrupted session and not a producer defect.\n- `cleanedUp=false` on the finished run, and the strand was stopped with `live=true` afterwards.\n\nSuggested fix: when a turn end is held for missing output files after an operator-started turn, send the agent a reminder or hand the pane back to the operator with a visible notice instead of waiting out the whole timeout.\nClassify a timeout that follows an operator interrupt as resumable, not as `producer-hard-failure`.\n\nProvenance: task `transient-tripout-overpull`, from `loom-halt.md`.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:24Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:33:24Z","design_file":"design-describe-held-turn-waits-timeout.md","decision_file":"","revision":"0176aec05e7a8936e73bbad111091298c21c751560771e3ba41804d9908acb86"},{"handle":"B10","slug":"finalize-transient-eof-fails","title":"Finalize halts as hard failure on a GitHub API EOF that lym marks transient","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["landing","shed"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #240](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/240).\n\nA `lym loom step` at the Finalize producer read a pull request's status from the GitHub API and got `unexpected EOF`.\nThe step was refused with `transient=github-api`, yet the run halted as `failed` with anomaly `producer-hard-failure`.\n\nQuoted from the trace:\n\n```\nlevel=WARN msg=\"shed: step refused\" kind=producer transient=github-api error=\"landingshed: Finalize: read the pull request's status: pullrequest: read pull request #54: Get \\\"https://api.github.com/repos/\u003cowner\u003e/\u003crepo\u003e/pulls/54\\\": unexpected EOF\"\n```\n\nHalt note text: `loom halted failed at Finalize`, `state: failed`, `anomaly: producer-hard-failure`, 15 history entries, all earlier steps done or skipped.\n\nThis is abnormal because lym itself labels the error transient, and a single dropped connection to the API then ends a run that had finished every earlier step.\nOther network failures in the same run (an unreachable origin before a push) were retried until they landed, so the Finalize read is inconsistent with them.\n\nSuggested fix: when a step is refused with a non-empty `transient` kind, retry it with backoff, or leave the run resumable, instead of recording `producer-hard-failure`.\nA failure of this kind should reach `failed` only after the retry budget is spent.\n\nProvenance: task `transient-tripout-overpull`, from `loom-halt-2.md`.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:27Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:33:27Z","design_file":"design-finalize-transient-eof-fails.md","decision_file":"","revision":"2c87d0b81f91a510e14c93cca13ba4c6c65527a62d82f745306abb6e4ad0febd"},{"handle":"B11","slug":"died-judge-spends-bounce-budget","title":"A died bouncer judge session spends Code-Bouncer's bounce budget","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["burler","shed"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #239](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/239).\n\nOn a flaky network, Code-Bouncer's judge session twice failed to start: shuttle logged 'provider never became ready; strand torn down', relaunched, and the second run finished at once with no transcript. shedadapters then logged 'bouncer judge run did not complete ... outcome=died', and the row stopped with 'bounce budget exhausted for Code-Bouncer'. The judge never gave a verdict, so no bounce happened, yet the run stalled until a manual `lym loom resume`.\n\nExpected: a judge that dies before giving a verdict is relaunched, or the row halts as an infrastructure failure, without spending the bounce budget meant for real verdicts.","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:30Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:33:30Z","design_file":"design-died-judge-spends-bounce-budget.md","decision_file":"","revision":"b57091a989bcddeb13de4f14204d994bb27582142b363529c44ba1af065fddf5"},{"handle":"B12","slug":"push-failures-mailed-one-by-one","title":"Transient push failures are mailed to the orch one by one instead of retried","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["fabric"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #238](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/238).\n\nOn a network that resets HTTPS connections now and then (git exits 128 with 'Recv failure: Connection reset by peer'), every failed background push sends the orch a creel message naming `lym fabric push` or `lym board sync` as the way forward. In one hour on one hub the orch got eight such messages, for the bolt branch main, task code branches and records branches, and each retry by hand succeeded at once or failed again only because the connection was still flaky.\n\nExpected: fabric retries a transport failure itself with backoff, and mails the orch only once retries are exhausted. One message then covers every branch still unpushed, rather than one message per push. The next pair push already carries a withheld code side, so a single summary would suffice.\n\nRelated: #193 (closed), which made runs retry transient fetch failures; pushes have no such retry.","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:34Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:33:34Z","design_file":"design-push-failures-mailed-one-by-one.md","decision_file":"","revision":"67cca1867b74bdae219ea9b6a4d65ddf9d3996194e190f6fc69016686c0ad5cb"},{"handle":"B13","slug":"gate-no-tier0-fix-round","title":"lym gate test answers that the hub declares no tier0, leaving a Burler fix round without a gate","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["gate","burler"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #237](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/237).\n\nA Burler code fix round ran `lym gate test ./\u003cmodule path\u003e` on a Go module and the verb answered that the hub declares no tier0.\nThe round therefore had no gate, and the agent ran `go vet` and `go test` on the module directly.\n\nThis is abnormal because the fix-round instructions end each round with the gate; a hub without a declared tier0 leaves the round with nothing to run, and the agent improvised a substitute.\n\nWhat to do: either have `lym gate test` fall back to a default tier when tier0 is undeclared, or make the refusal name the config key to add, and have the Burler code prompt say what to run when no gate exists.\n\nProvenance: task slug timesheet-suggest-tool; notes burler-code-r1.md.","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:38Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:33:38Z","design_file":"design-gate-no-tier0-fix-round.md","decision_file":"","revision":"f1a6054578dcd78ee5b8e0b3ebf51772cc7597384b40a643b18d0eecc5f37b5d"},{"handle":"B14","slug":"beam-orch-cannot-amend-record","title":"Beam orch cannot amend its own beam's decision record","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["loom","orch","pawl"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #236](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/236).\n\n## What happened\n\nIn hub `dop`, beam `models-torque-drag` (P1) holds a stored decision record.\nOn 2026-11-24 the operator made a decision that supersedes two of its decisions: a new member task maps the well's formation strength into the Models bit-rock law, where the beam record's D1 and D2 keep Models' bit-rock defaults.\nThe member's Discussion saw the conflict and asked the beam orch, which owns the beam record, to settle it.\n\nThe beam orch could not amend its own beam's record:\n\n- `lym loom decision add models-torque-drag --by parent ...` from the beam orch refused: `the caller \"dop:models-torque-drag:orch\" is a task session; way forward: a design call after the Discussion is the parent's to record; ask dop:orch to run the verb`.\n  The beam orch is an orch, not a task session, and it is the record's owner.\n- `lym board upsert --record-file` refuses an entry a run holds, and a beam's project is always held by its pawl run (`running · Beam-Await`), so that way is closed for the beam's whole life.\n\n## Expected\n\nThe beam's own orch can append a decision to, or replace, its beam's decision record while the beam lives, as a run's parent can for a task's record.\nThe caller check counts a beam orch (`\u003chub\u003e:\u003cbeam\u003e:orch`) as an orch, not as a task session.\n\n## Related\n\nThe same misclassification of the beam orch shows in parent-review: `lym loom parent-review answer` refuses the beam orch for its member's pull request (`neither the run's parent orch nor the strand the ask went to`), filed by the hub orch as Knatte18/loomyard#232.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:42Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:33:42Z","design_file":"design-beam-orch-cannot-amend-record.md","decision_file":"","revision":"ab497ea277a5bb3fae37e6ea7fcda233f5c7e01e4038940a4ac0669e993bbf02"},{"handle":"B15","slug":"gate-detailed-logger-no-test","title":"gate test: reports 'ran no test' when the detailed console logger's summary is used","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["gate","dotnet"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #235](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/235).\n\nWith `--logger \"console;verbosity=detailed\"` passed to `lym gate test`, the gate reported that it ran no test, although the test log shows `Total tests: 1, Passed: 1`. The gate's test counter appears to misread the detailed logger's summary format.\n\nThis is abnormal because a passing run is reported as a run of nothing, which an agent may read as a failure or retry needlessly. The counter should parse the summary lines of every console logger verbosity, or read the result from a structured logger (trx) instead.\n\nProvenance: task models-stick-slip-floor-tune, note Darn-Write.md.","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:45Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:33:45Z","design_file":"design-gate-detailed-logger-no-test.md","decision_file":"","revision":"95ce0fe7c69e26eebaadca3d586be3fdfd0262e4d10574e65ca035c456b2deba"},{"handle":"B16","slug":"gate-tier3-double-filter","title":"gate test: tier3 command already carries --filter, so a second --filter in args makes dotnet test refuse","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["gate","dotnet"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #234](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/234).\n\nThe tier3 command of `lym gate test` already carries a `--filter`. Passing a test filter in `{args}` adds a second `--filter`, and `dotnet test` refuses the call. An agent found that `-p:VSTestTestCaseFilter=...` works as an override, but nothing in the verb's help says so.\n\nThis is abnormal because selecting one test class is the common use of the verb. The gate should merge a caller filter into the tier's filter (or replace it) and document the way to do it.\n\nProvenance: task models-stick-slip-floor-tune, note Darn-Write.md.","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:49Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:33:49Z","design_file":"design-gate-tier3-double-filter.md","decision_file":"","revision":"739d20fb33723a59548fe39ad7ca3d706de7a50a8bdc5bbfef14347db5d16def"},{"handle":"B17","slug":"darn-gate-key-tier0-only","title":"gate test: darn-write gate key runs tier0 only, so a tier3 measurement test runs nothing","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["gate","darn"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #233](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/233).\n\nA prompt told an agent to run a measurement test through `lym gate test --gate darn-write`. That gate key runs tier0 only, while the test lived in tier3 (a slow-test project, category `ModelsLong`). The first call ran no test, and the orch had to allow `--tier 3` by hand.\n\nThis is abnormal because the prompt and the gate key disagree on the tier. Either the prompt generator should pass `--tier` when the named test is not tier0, or the gate should say, when it runs no test, that the filter matches nothing at this tier and name the tiers that would match.\n\nProvenance: task models-stick-slip-floor-tune, note Darn-Write.md.","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:52Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:33:52Z","design_file":"design-darn-gate-key-tier0-only.md","decision_file":"","revision":"7d9d89cfb26290548aef4c737e3dd0cf5e097b105bc69e5e0364a9f4d3fb05fa"},{"handle":"B18","slug":"beam-member-parent-is-prime","title":"A beam member's run records the prime orch as its parent orch","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["batten","landing","orch"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #232](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/232).\n\n## Seen\n\nIn the DrillOpPlan hub on 2026-11-24, the run `models-stick-slip-map`, a member of the beam `models-torque-drag` and spawned from the beam's worktree, records the prime's orch `dop:orch` as its parent orch.\nIts PR-Review sent the `[pr-review request]` for PR #52 (into the beam branch `hanf/models-torque-drag`) to `dop:orch`, as ask 1 from the PR-Review session and as ask 2 from `dop:LOOM`.\nThe beam's orch `dop:models-torque-drag:orch`, which owns and released the run, reviewed the PR, and its answer was refused:\n\n```\ncaller dop:models-torque-drag:orch is neither the run's parent orch nor the strand the ask went to\n```\n\nThe beam's dobby sends its notices for the same run to `dop:orch` too.\nKnatte18/loomyard#231 shows the same gap in the tackler and driver prompts of a beam member.\n\n## Expected\n\nA beam member's run records the beam's orch as its parent orch, so its parent-review asks, notices and escalations go to the beam's orch and that orch's answer is accepted.\n\n## Workaround\n\nThe prime orch forwards each ask and notice to the beam's orch, and runs `lym loom parent-review answer \u003cslug\u003e --ask \u003cn\u003e --satisfied` in the task worktree on the beam orch's verdict.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:55Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:33:55Z","design_file":"design-beam-member-parent-is-prime.md","decision_file":"","revision":"a45587b690f061ea0880d52d94852d488c4893a8a7b4a69e9dde10d83eb9439f"},{"handle":"B19","slug":"beam-member-prompt-no-parent","title":"Tackler and driver prompts of a beam member say no parent is recorded","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["stencil","shed"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #231](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/231).\n\n## Seen\n\nIn the DrillOpPlan hub on 2026-11-10, the shed tackler of run `models-backend`, a member of the beam `adopt-models-sim`, got a prompt whose section \"Your parent\" read:\n\n```\nNo parent is recorded for this run.\nWhen you are blocked, write your report and stop.\n```\n\nThe run had a parent: the beam's orch `dop:adopt-models-sim:orch`, which owned and released it.\nOther tackler and driver prompts in the hub carried the same text, in the form \"No parent is recorded for the run, so the question is written for whoever owns it.\"\nThe tackler escalated on a merge conflict and stopped; the prime orch saw the stop and passed it to the beam orch by hand.\n\n## Expected\n\nThe tackler and driver prompts of a beam member name the beam's orch as the parent, so a blocked tackler or driver reaches it directly.\n\n## Workaround\n\nThe prime orch forwards a beam member's stop to the beam's orch by creel.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:58Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:33:58Z","design_file":"design-beam-member-prompt-no-parent.md","decision_file":"","revision":"255ecbca37e89673d5460c7efbe4646620c64d3b5689d25fc28796b8b8fe3317"},{"handle":"B21","slug":"pr-body-not-synced","title":"The GitHub PR body is not updated when the description file changes","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["landing"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #228](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/228).\n\n## Seen\n\nAn analysis of the parent's PR-Review findings in the C# hubs found that the same findings were sent again in about five rounds.\nThe agent fixed the description file but the pull request's body on GitHub was not updated, so the parent read the old text again.\nSeen in five separate rounds across several runs.\n\n## Why it makes no sense\n\nThe description file is the one source of the PR title and body.\nA PR whose body differs from the file the run holds is wrong from the first push, and a review round is spent on text that has already been fixed.\n\n## Idea\n\nMake the PR body follow the description file on every push, and refuse to end a round while the two differ.\n\n- Read first where `pr update` runs for a task's pull request, and which path writes the description file without calling it (`lym beam describe` runs the `pr update` sequence for a beam; check the task path).\n- After each push, compare the GitHub PR body with the file, and update it or halt with a way forward naming the difference.\n- Add a test that edits the description file after the PR exists and expects the body to follow on the next push.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:34:08Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:34:08Z","design_file":"design-pr-body-not-synced.md","decision_file":"","revision":"c9c58d022dcf8f1c61e8475c828ec1d0c124bff12868d4090d1d8feff97f3679"},{"handle":"B22","slug":"webster-plan-fix-begun-batch","title":"Plan fixes after begun batches block Webster; glyph-not-found on members a card creates","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["webster","quarry"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #221](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/221).\n\n## What happened\n\nTwo Webster halts after the plan was edited by a plan review/fix round while batches were already begun.\n\n1. `blocked` / `escalation-to-human`:\n\n```\nwebster: the auto-rebaseline of the changed plan blocks this run: webster: the edited plan changes the cards of a batch this run already begun: 00-overview.md changed outside its Card Index; it carries the plan's integration verify and only its Card Index is rebaselined; way forward: restore 00-overview.md, or fix the plan: move a done card's edit to a follow-up card after the last begun batch that carries the decision, with its Card Index line in 00-overview.md, then re-step the Webster row\n```\n\n2. After the plan was fixed and the run re-stepped, `failed` / `producer-hard-failure`, plan validation refused with four `glyph-not-found` findings, all of the form:\n\n```\nglyph-not-found/\u003ccard\u003e[blocking]: target \"\u003cUnit\u003e#\u003cMember\u003e\"'s unit exists but the member is missing — check for a misspelled member\n```\n\nThe members named were ones the plan's cards (5 and 6) introduce or modify, and the targets include a method signature with a generic parameter type (`IReadOnlyList\u003cdouble\u003e`) and a nullable (`TimeSpan?`).\nCard 5 and card 6 cite the same new member as a target.\n\n## Why abnormal\n\n- The plan review loop (`Plan-Burler-Fix`) edited `00-overview.md` outside its Card Index and the run then blocked on it; the plan-fix stage gives no warning that such an edit is unrecoverable once a batch has begun, so each such fix costs an operator escalation.\n- If the glyph targets name members that an earlier card of the same plan creates, validation cannot find them at plan time and refuses a valid plan. If they are misspelled, the finding text does not say which member of the unit is closest. Either way the finding should say whether the member is expected to be created by a card.\n  I could not tell from the notes which case applies.\n\n## What to do\n\n- Have the plan-fix stage refuse or warn on edits to `00-overview.md` outside the Card Index once any batch has begun.\n- Let glyph validation accept a target that an earlier card of the plan declares as created, and print the unit's actual member names beside a `glyph-not-found` finding.\n\nProvenance: task `models-torque-drag-adapter`; notes `loom-halt-4.md`, `loom-halt-6.md`.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:34:29Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:34:29Z","design_file":"design-webster-plan-fix-begun-batch.md","decision_file":"","revision":"85db7e25fd75d09e6e579264d88368762627b6123c96cb87508aa03cd9e8ab44"},{"handle":"B23","slug":"webster-recovery-asking-dead","title":"Webster recovery ending dead with dead_reason asking drops the question and needs a manual reset","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["webster"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #220](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/220).\n\n## What happened\n\nA Webster batch's verify failed on a test the plan could not satisfy.\nThe batch recovery session ended `dead` with `dead_reason: asking`, i.e. the recovery agent stopped to ask a question instead of finishing, and `recoveries: 1`, `recovery_retry: false`.\nThen:\n\n```\nlym webster recover-batch 3 refused:\nwebster: batch 03-torque-drag-stage recovery (session ...) ended dead and the batch has no recovery left; way forward: end the run stuck, naming batch 03-torque-drag-stage\n```\n\nThe run halted `blocked` (anomaly `escalation-to-human`).\nAfter the plan was edited and rebaselined, Webster halted `blocked` again:\n\n```\nwebster: a batch of this run is at a dead end: batch 03-torque-drag-stage is dead and its recovery earns no retry, so a Master would only end the run stuck on it; way forward: 1) lym webster reset --to batch-start --batch 03; 2) re-step the Webster row; or 1) lym webster reset --to start; 2) re-step the Webster row\n```\n\n## Why abnormal\n\nA recovery agent that asks a question has no channel that answers it, so the question becomes a dead recovery with no retry.\nThe agent's question, which would tell the operator what is wrong, is not carried into the halt text: the halt note only restates \"dead_reason: asking\".\nPlan edits made to fix the cause did not clear the dead state either; a manual `webster reset` was needed.\n\n## What to do\n\n- When a recovery ends with `dead_reason: asking`, put the agent's last message in the halt/refusal note.\n- Either route the question to the parent (`lym creel post --parent --expect-reply`) or let a plan rebaseline that touches the dead batch's cards re-arm one recovery, so the operator does not need a reset.\n\nProvenance: task `models-torque-drag-adapter`; notes `loom-halt.md`, `loom-halt-5.md`, `webster-refusal-recover-batch.md`.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:34:32Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:34:32Z","design_file":"design-webster-recovery-asking-dead.md","decision_file":"","revision":"1203b437c06b70c2d0a8192020fab23cf7e545a6d6c7cc61018cc013156f469e"},{"handle":"B24","slug":"seat-idle-no-input-box","title":"Shuttle idle check reports no input box on an idle seat, killing Plan-Burler-Review","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["shuttle","claude"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #219](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/219).\n\n## What happened\n\nThe `Plan-Burler-Review` row (`burler-review`) halted `failed` (anomaly `producer-hard-failure`) after round 3: two consecutive `died` outcomes, both \"review never started\".\nEach attempt's seat start ended with:\n\n```\nshuttle: deliver the prompt after loading skills: shuttle: the session is not idle: strand \"\u003cid\u003e\": no input box is on screen; a menu or picker may be open; retry once the session is idle.\n```\n\nThe pane lines quoted in the same error show the opposite: the skill-load reply `ok scribe:prose, scribe:code-quality, scribe:testing` had finished, a `done` status line was printed, and the `❯` input prompt plus the `bypass permissions on` footer were on screen.\nThe session was idle with an input box; the idle check reported none.\nIt happened on attempts 3 and 3b, in two separate halts, with the same pane text each time (`Sautéed for 3s`, `Cogitated for 3s`, `Cooked for 2s` status lines).\n\n## Why abnormal\n\nThe seat was healthy.\nA false \"no input box\" reading kills a seat that never got its prompt, burns the review rounds, and halts the whole run as `failed`.\nThe error text itself says \"retry once the session is idle\", yet the shuttle did not retry after the capture showed an idle pane.\n\n## What to do\n\nCheck the input-box detection in the shuttle's deliver-after-skills step against a pane whose last lines are a completed skill-load reply, a `✻ \u003cverb\u003e for Ns · done` line, the `❯` prompt and the permissions footer.\nRetry the idle check for a short window before declaring `died`, so a repaint race right after the skill-load reply does not kill the seat.\n\nProvenance: task `models-torque-drag-adapter`; notes `loom-halt-2.md`, `loom-halt-3.md`.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:34:35Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:34:35Z","design_file":"design-seat-idle-no-input-box.md","decision_file":"","revision":"5bd6a1449fff036ba4153cbe14547dfab2f763321b827d371b9da07ee99733a4"},{"handle":"B25","slug":"seed-ide-push-not-landed","title":"Seed-IDE fails the run when the task branch's detached push has not landed yet","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["batten","fabric"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #218](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/218).\n\nImport note: the issue names files under `.lym/`, which is deleted with its worktree, so only what the issue quotes of them reached the board: `.lym/logs`.\n\n## What happened\n\nRun `weekly-time-report` (recipe raddle, promoted from a Sketch) stopped at Seed-IDE with:\n\n\u003e run \"weekly-time-report\" cannot act: the code tip e7b0cc2 the run's last transition names is not on origin yet; way forward: run \"lym batten run weekly-time-report\" again once the code push lands\n\nTimeline from `.lym/logs` (2026-11-24, CET):\n\n- 14:45:52 Worktree-Create done; the task branch forks from the prime's local `main` at e7b0cc2, one commit ahead of `origin/main`.\n- 14:45:52 and 14:45:55 fabricengine spawned detached pushes of the new pair (`fabric --warp-path …/weekly-time-report …`).\n- 14:45:57 Seed-IDE's bootstrap checked whether e7b0cc2 is on origin, found it was not, and refused the step.\n- Shortly after, the detached push landed: `git ls-remote origin` showed `refs/heads/weekly-time-report` at e7b0cc2.\n\nThe network was slow and resetting that day (`Recv failure: Connection reset by peer` on records pushes), so the detached push took longer than the 2–5 s gap.\n\n## Effect\n\n- The run stopped as failed, and the tackler handed it back to the parent asking whether to push the prime's `main`, which was not needed.\n- A plain `lym batten run weekly-time-report` afterwards went straight through Seed-IDE to Run-Shed.\n\n## Expected\n\nA check that the code tip is on origin, right after lym itself spawned the push that puts it there, should wait for that push, or retry for a while, instead of failing the run.\nThe stop should also say that lym's own push of the task branch is in flight, so the tackler does not ask the parent to push the parent branch.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:34:38Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:34:38Z","design_file":"design-seed-ide-push-not-landed.md","decision_file":"","revision":"94a5621d4e97d55d020a66bfd2fec9f48b079aa4864d5a78c3fcf66aa992ffee"},{"handle":"B26","slug":"gate-timeout-hung-testhost","title":"Gate tier timeout_min does not stop a hung dotnet testhost; the slot stayed held 21 hours","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["gate","dotnet"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #216](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/216).\n\n## What happened\n\nIn hub `dop`, run `models-torque-drag-adapter`, a Webster batch fork ran `lym gate test --tier tier3` at 2026-11-23 15:36.\nTier3 has `timeout_min: 35` in the gate config.\nThe tier's `dotnet test` testhost hung (about 4.5 min CPU in 21 h), and the gate slot stayed held for nearly 21 hours: `lym gate status` showed the slot held since 15:36 the next day.\nThe 35-minute bound never fired, so the fork, Webster and the run waited without any stop or notice to the parent orch.\nAnother hub's orch noticed the idle process; the parent orch killed the testhost by hand, and the gate then released its slot.\n\n## Expected\n\nAt `timeout_min` the gate kills the tier's whole process tree (`sh -c dotnet test` → `dotnet` → `vstest` → testhost) and reports a timeout failure to its caller.\n\n## Likely area\n\nThe timeout may kill only the direct child (`sh`), or wait on output pipes that the orphaned testhost still holds open, so the wait never returns.\n\n\n## From issue #215\n\nImported from [issue #215](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/215).\n\nOn a Models hub, `lym gate test --wait --gate finalize` (PID 3536339) ran tier1, `dotnet test NORCE.Models.SlowTests -p:RunSlowTests=true` with `timeout_min: 30`, from 2026-11-23 15:36 to the next day, over 20 h. The test host had used about 7 min CPU; it was hung. The gate slot stayed held the whole time and the run sat at Finalize. On the same machine a DrillOpPlan hub's tier3 gate, started the same minute with a 35-minute bound, hung the same way and its bound never fired either. The machine may have been suspended overnight, but the bound did not fire before that either (15:36 + 30 min). Expected: the gate kills the tier's process tree at timeout_min and reports a timeout.","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:34:45Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:34:48Z","design_file":"design-gate-timeout-hung-testhost.md","decision_file":"","revision":"778d2c6cbce7ec07883f84023e8882ab2fcaa2d08cbe40828c062f54149707e6"},{"handle":"E1","slug":"periodic-pair-push","title":"Periodic async push of pairs whatever made the commits","kind":"note","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["fabric","dobby"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Enhancements","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #247](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/247).\n\n## Idea\n\nAgents should only have to commit; they should never have to think about pushing.\nlym takes care of every push itself, asynchronously, without blocking an agent.\n\n## Seen\n\nlym already pushes asynchronously after `lym fabric commit`, but only then.\nAn agent that commits with plain `git commit` leaves its commits unpushed until some later lym step pushes the pair, and the push failures that follow land on whichever step happens to push next.\n\n## Proposal\n\nA periodic pusher in lym, for example in the dobby or its own small daemon, that pushes each live pair's code and records branches when they are ahead of origin, however the commits were made.\n\n- Periodic and not aggressive: a fixed interval of minutes, not a push per commit.\n- Never blocks an agent or a row; a failed push is retried at the next period, with backoff, and reported once rather than per attempt.\n- Rows that need origin current, such as Finalize, still push synchronously as now.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:02Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:33:02Z","design_file":"design-periodic-pair-push.md","decision_file":"","revision":"cb0110555680395a7dc78128e993771a12b9b83e5fed3930aec942e4f89d6d3a"},{"handle":"E2","slug":"writers-commit-each-step","title":"Iterative agents should commit often","kind":"note","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["stencil","darn"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Enhancements","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #246](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/246).\n\n## Seen\n\nA darn writer worked through a multi-step change, editing seven files over a long stretch, without committing until its parent asked.\nAn earlier writer in the same hub stalled on an API error and then died in a machine reboot with its work uncommitted, so only the untracked files survived in the worktree.\n\n## Expected\n\nAgents that work iteratively, darn writers above all, commit after each coherent step instead of holding the whole change uncommitted until the end.\nThe writer stencils and skills say so, so that a stall, a dead strand or a reboot loses at most one step, and the parent can see progress in the branch's history.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:06Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:33:06Z","design_file":"design-writers-commit-each-step.md","decision_file":"","revision":"667c3532cec1320c896a530fa32bcfa3be3e4ffe64815d261adaf4c2eaadf599"},{"handle":"E3","slug":"keep-landed-records-in-weft","title":"Keep the decision record and review findings of each landed task in the weft","kind":"note","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["landing","fabric","report"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Enhancements","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #229](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/229).\n\nImport note: the issue names files under `.lym/`, which is deleted with its worktree, so only what the issue quotes of them reached the board: `_bolt/.lym/creel/journal`.\n\n## Seen (operator, 2026-11-24)\n\nAn analysis of the parent's PR-Review findings in the C# hubs could not finish its work, because the evidence was gone.\nAbout 29 findings were in scratchpad files under `/tmp` that no longer exist.\nThe decision records (Sketches) of the landed tasks could not be read: the board holds only open entries, and the task worktrees are deleted.\nSo the analysis could not say whether delivered code broke its decision record, and every gate attribution was inferred.\nThe only complete source left was each hub's creel mail journal under `_bolt/.lym/creel/journal`.\n\nThe operator wants this kept: absolutely.\n\n## Idea\n\nAt landing, store what a later study needs in the weft, where it survives the worktree's deletion.\n\n- The decision record with its `Added after Discussion` entries, as it stood at landing.\n- Every Code-Review round's findings and the fixer reports, the parent's rulings and answers, and the PR-Review findings.\n- The Describe text as it was merged, and the drift verdict.\n- A short index row per landed task, so `lym report` can read them without walking directories.\n- Read first which records survive today and where, and how landing already archives a run (the archive tag, `Reconcile leftover shuttle runs from run records`).\n- Decide the weft path and how long records are kept, and make sure findings no longer live only in a scratchpad directory.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:34:04Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:34:04Z","design_file":"design-keep-landed-records-in-weft.md","decision_file":"","revision":"9255067586bbbd4d84f0e5052dcb3a26c6e057b5d0a724115f8ee2f3e6c1d0b0"},{"handle":"E4","slug":"describe-no-loomyard-terms","title":"The Describe text names no LoomYard terms, except the Created using block","kind":"note","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["landing","stencil"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Enhancements","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #227](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/227).\n\n## Seen (operator, 2026-11-24)\n\nAn analysis of the parent's PR-Review findings in the C# hubs found that about 12 findings were internal process or tool names in the PR text: \"item 3 of the task\", \"the test scoper cannot run C# tests there\", \"the task's branch point\", \"The board entry proposed\", \"The parent recorded...\".\nThe stencil that writes the description (`landing-template-describe`) does not forbid them, so the parent had to tell the Describe session so in each hub.\nIn one run the parent noted its own earlier instruction had caused it.\n\nThe operator's rule: LoomYard, and anything from it, does not belong in the Describe text.\nThe one exception is the \"Created using\" block at the bottom.\n\n## Idea\n\nKeep LoomYard's vocabulary out of the description the Describe row writes, and let lym hold it.\n\n- Add the rule to the Describe stencil: the description names no board, task, Sketch, decision record, parent, orch, scoper, gate, tier, perch or lym term, and no \"item N of the task\".\n- Let the Describe row's mechanical gate (`NewDescriptionGate`, which runs `summaryparser.ValidateDescription`) refuse such a term outside the credit block, with the refusal naming the term and the line.\n- Leave the credit block as it is: the `credit` keys in `landing.yaml` append it, and the term check skips it.\n- Read first how the credit block is appended to the description, and which words the check can match without refusing a repository's own legitimate names.\n- The issue on reviewing the Describe text covers claims and missing content; this issue covers the vocabulary.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:34:11Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:34:11Z","design_file":"design-describe-no-loomyard-terms.md","decision_file":"","revision":"93d1ac973c346df9b148b4883b765becb67ed9ba314cc6c773bbd3454a2fd5ba"},{"handle":"E5","slug":"scope-closes-at-code-review","title":"Close the scope of a task when its first Code-Review round starts, and have lym hold the line","kind":"note","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["loom","burler"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Enhancements","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #226](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/226).\n\n## Seen (operator, 2026-11-24)\n\nA task's scope can still change late.\nIn the C# hubs a parent orch recorded decisions that widened a task while the run was at PR-Review, after Code-Review had finished, and the reviewers never judged the diff against the widened record.\nIn one run Code-Review stopped at its round cap because a decision finding spawns no fixer, so the widening was in the record but never built.\n\nThe operator's rule: decisions go in as early as possible, and once the Code-Review perch has begun it is too late.\nThe last Code-Review round is the last link that can change anything.\nWhat comes up after that becomes a note for a new task, not a change to this one.\n\n## Idea\n\nClose a task's scope when its first Code-Review round starts, and have lym hold the line, so it is not only a rule for the parent orch.\n\n- Before the line, a decision enters the record as an `Added after Discussion` entry, and `code-scope` and the plan lenses read it.\n- After the line, no decision enters the record.\n  `lym loom decision add` (and `replace`) refuse once the run stands at or past the first Code-Review round, and the refusal names the way forward: file the call as a note for a new task.\n  The refusal reaches the parent and the agent the way the other `decision add` refusals do.\n- Two things stay outside the line: feedback from a person through the PR agent, and the mechanical fixes Finalize makes to a merge or test failure.\n- The orch procedure and the parent's prompt say the same, so the parent files a note instead of trying.\n- Read first how `lym loom decision add` decides where a run stands (`internal/loomcli/decision.go` already refuses at some stages), where the Drift rows sit against the Code-Review perch, whether a Drift verdict can send a run back past the line, and how darn, tapestry and arras recipes place their Code-Review perch.\n- Related issues: taking the parent out of PR-Review, and reviewing the Describe text and catching the rest of the parent's findings earlier.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:34:14Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:34:14Z","design_file":"design-scope-closes-at-code-review.md","decision_file":"","revision":"6d4eacf9dd817cc92355429bba3c6503a59edd041b11c52b8facfe7f9863d72e"},{"handle":"E6","slug":"review-describe-text","title":"Review the Describe text inside the Describe row, and catch the rest of what the parent finds at PR-Review earlier","kind":"note","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["landing","burler"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Enhancements","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #225](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/225).\n\n## Seen (operator, 2026-11-24)\n\nIn the C# repos on one machine, a parent asked to review a PR always finds something.\nThat points to a design flaw: if the parent finds something, an earlier quality check missed it.\n\nThe operator's guess was that the delivered code often does not cover everything the Discussion's decision record asked for.\nThe operator judges the finding below, that most of it is the PR text, as not very important, and that it is solved by reviewing the Describe text too.\n\n## What the parent finds (analysis, 2026-11-24)\n\nAn analysis read 25 PR-Review rounds with findings, across four hubs; one more hub had no PR-Review records.\nAbout 65 findings are itemised in readable text; about 29 more were in scratchpad files that no longer exist.\nThe task worktrees and the Sketches of the landed tasks are gone, so the only full source is each hub's creel mail journal, and the gate attributions are mostly inferred.\nThe full report is a local file and is not committed.\n\n- About half of the findings (32 of 65) are about the PR description, not the code:\n  internal process or tool names in the text (12), wrong or overreaching claims (12), and content the task asked for that the text omits (8).\n- The rest are tests missing or too costly (10), wrong behaviour (8), doc or comment drift (7) and scope the Sketch did not hold (8).\n- The operator's guess holds only in part: 4 of 25 rounds cite a Sketch or task requirement that was missed, and all four concern the PR text or scope, not code behaviour.\n- A finding is often sent twice because the agent edits the summary file but not the GitHub PR body (about 5 rounds).\n\n## Idea\n\nThe Describe row writes the change description, which becomes the PR title, the PR body and the landing commit message.\nIt is a seat table (`DescribeTable` in `internal/landingshed/describe.go`) whose session already reads the decision record, and its only gate is mechanical (`ValidateDescription`); no agent reviews what the text says.\n\n1. Review the Describe text inside the Describe row with a second agent, an assistant seat, and send its findings back to the writer; Describe is the last step that uses an LLM, so no review runs after it.\n   The reviewer checks that the text names no internal process or tool (board, task, scoper, Sketch, decision record, \"item N of\"), that every claim holds against the diff and the task's report or measurements, and that every deliverable the Sketch says to state in the description is there.\n   The mechanical gate stays in front of it.\n   This covers the 32 findings about the text.\n2. Sync the GitHub PR body from the description on every push, and verify the two match (about 5 re-sent rounds).\n3. Run the mutation or redundancy tier on every C# task, and give the Plan a tier0 cost budget (10 test findings).\n4. Have Discussion record what the parent will check: measured figures, doc statements and test intent.\n5. Let a Code-Review finding the parent already ruled in scope reach a fixer; today a decision finding spawns none.\n6. Re-check a constant quoted in a doc when the case it was measured on changes.\n\nThe issue on taking the parent out of PR-Review leaves these checks to the cold-start reviewers, so they must run before PR-Review, and a task's scope closes at its first Code-Review round (see the issue on closing a task's scope).\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:34:17Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:34:17Z","design_file":"design-review-describe-text.md","decision_file":"","revision":"bc2ffded4aa97867717ee1b4c561b0c116159eddefbbbdf49462365847a4f374"},{"handle":"E7","slug":"parent-out-of-pr-review","title":"Take the parent out of PR-Review; the earlier reviewers must catch what it finds","kind":"note","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["landing","loom"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Enhancements","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #223](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/223).\n\n## Seen (operator, 2026-11-24)\n\nToday the parent orch can be asked to review at PR-Review, and the operator has used that a lot.\nThe operator now judges it a bad idea, for two reasons.\n\nPR-Review is the very last gate, and it belongs to the human.\nWhen a run reaches it, everything the LLMs and LoomYard can do should already be done.\n\nA parent is also the wrong reviewer.\nAsking the parent, which is the orchestrator, to review a Discussion or code is work for the orchestrator, and it costs more than a cold-start agent.\nThe reviewers a run already has should catch what the parent catches.\n\n## Decision (operator, 2026-11-26)\n\nNo agent reviews the pull request any more: when the PR is made, the agents are done.\nWhat a parent finds when asked to review a PR must be found and fixed before the PR;\nthe checks that catch it are in the notes on reviewing the Describe text and on closing a task's scope at Code-Review.\n\n## Idea\n\nTake every agent out of PR-Review and add no seat in its place.\n\n- Remove the parent's ask and re-ask from PR-Review in `internal/landingshed` and `internal/loomcli`.\n- Remove PR-Review's own interactive session and its findings path: `pr_reviewer`, `lym loom pr-findings`, `pr_review_small_max`, `pr_review_ask_parent` and `pr_review_parent_timeout_min`.\n- The row keeps only the mechanics the PR mode needs (see the note on the PR mode's three settings).\n- Rely on the cold-start reviewers.\n  The `review-code` fan already holds `code-scope`, which traces every decision of the record to a change in the diff and flags a decision no change builds, besides `code-correctness`, `code-affected-set` and `code-tests`.\n  Read first which lenses the C# hubs run in `review-code` today, since the hub's own `lenses.yaml` and the C# layer can change the fan.\n- Decide what a run does with a commit pushed to the PR branch by hand while it waits.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:34:23Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:59:27Z","design_file":"design-parent-out-of-pr-review.md","decision_file":"","revision":"53fb29055c57ad8c9fb0f9cf0e6e5b7160c755ac881860605a888ec4431b82d2"},{"handle":"E8","slug":"autonomy-pr-review-operator","title":"Autonomy: let PR review stay with the operator for one beam","kind":"note","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["landing","config"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Enhancements","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #217](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/217).\n\n`beam.yaml: autonomy` is one switch for both halts and PR-Review requests. A hub wants the orch to settle halts on the prime autonomously while pull requests into the prime's branch (main) still wait for the operator's approval. Today that needs autonomy off, which also sends every halt to the operator, or autonomy on with the orch holding PR-Review by hand. Proposal: a separate key, such as `autonomy_pr_review: false`, so PR-Review requests on that beam or the prime go to the operator while halts stay autonomous.\n\n## Issue comment, 2026-11-24\n\nSeen again, with a merge into the prime's branch:\n\n- The prime runs with `autonomy: true`, and the hub wants pull requests into main to wait for a human approval.\n- The task `import-project-names` reached PR-Review; the parent answered the parent-review ask `--satisfied` and told the PR-Review agent by creel that landing waits on the operator's approval.\n- `lym loom pr-continue` ran anyway about a minute later, and Finalize squash-merged the pull request into main once its gate passed, with no human approval on GitHub.\n\nA message to the PR-Review agent is no way to hold a landing under autonomy: the satisfied answer alone ends the review.\n\nExpected: a setting, per beam and for the prime, that keeps autonomy for halts but makes PR-Review wait for a human approval of the pull request (for example a GitHub approving review) before `pr-continue` or Finalize may merge.","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:34:42Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:34:42Z","design_file":"design-autonomy-pr-review-operator.md","decision_file":"","revision":"9cac83f8736fbc72d65e384e24d04d42255fd2bb04aa20c3786158272fc588aa"},{"handle":"F1","slug":"pr-auto-approve-mode","title":"A PR mode that registers the PR and approves it at once, with no agent under the block","kind":"note","types":["feature"],"areas":["landing","config"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Features","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #224](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/224).\n\n## Seen (operator, 2026-11-24)\n\nThe operator often wants a pull request registered on GitHub without looking at it.\nWith a beam, say P1, and several tasks under it, the operator wants each task to land as a PR that is approved at once and merged into the beam.\nWhen all of P1 is done, one PR goes from P1 into its parent (P0 or root), and it lists every member PR.\nThat lets the operator backtrack and see how the work got where it is.\n\n## Decision (operator, 2026-11-26)\n\nThe PR block has three modes, and no LLM takes part in any of them:\n\n- Manual: the PR is made and waits for the operator's own review and approval, as PR-Review is meant today.\n- None: no PR; the row passes, as `pull_request` off does today.\n- Trail (`pull_request: trail`, operator 2026-11-26): the PR is made and merged at once, with no agent and no human, so the beam's PR history can be backtracked.\n\nHow the three modes are configured is not decided yet.\n\n## Idea\n\n- Trail: the PR is made, no agent is spawned, and Finalize merges it at once, as it does today after the operator's `lym loom pr-continue`; merging a PR without an approval has long worked in lym.\n- A beam's own PR lists its member PRs.\n  `Rewrite a beam's pull request description after each member landing` (`1cd11ee5e`) may already do part of this; read what it writes before adding more.\n- Manual waits for a human approval whatever the autonomy, which covers the note on autonomy and PR-Review.\n- Read first where `beam.yaml` `pull_request` is read (`internal/landingshed`, `internal/pullrequest`) and how a run reaches Finalize today when a PR is approved.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:34:20Z","revised":"2026-11-26T08:09:44Z","design_file":"design-pr-auto-approve-mode.md","decision_file":"","revision":"96e2a2e579c1d1d5622f5bb810624c0ae95c172833be979820f6e3aa8996e8e2"},{"handle":"F2","slug":"dobby-run-watch","title":"Built-in run watch: mail the parent on every non-running state and on a gate slot held too long","kind":"note","types":["feature"],"areas":["dobby","gate"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Features","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Imported from [issue #222](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/222).\n\n## What the orch does by hand today\n\nIn hub `dop`, the operator requires the orch to hear of every stop of every run it parents.\nlym notifies some halts, but not all: a Webster block sends no notice (#207), and a gate whose testhost hangs past `timeout_min` holds its slot with no notice at all (#216).\n\nSo the orch keeps a Claude Code Monitor per run in flight: a shell loop that polls `lym loom status` in the task worktree every 30 s, prints on every change of state or producer, and prints a warning when `lym gate status` shows the run holding a gate slot past 45 minutes.\nThe Monitor expires after 30 minutes, so the orch re-arms it by hand, and a missed re-arm left a hung tier3 gate unseen for 21 hours.\n\n## Wanted\n\nlym itself watches every run in flight and mails its parent (creel) on:\n\n- every state other than running: blocked, failed, paused, done, whatever the cause;\n- a gate slot held by the run past a bound, such as 1.5 × the tier's `timeout_min`, naming the gate's PID and its child processes;\n- optionally, every row change, for parents that want progress.\n\nThe watch lives in the dobby, not in the orch's session, so it needs no re-arming and survives the orch's context cycles.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-26T07:34:26Z","revised":"2026-11-26T07:34:26Z","design_file":"design-dobby-run-watch.md","decision_file":"","revision":"b7c4bf8fb8aa0ffb5bf1d9b7bf7539db52ac7a85e0c54705be5162966c546138"},{"handle":"F9","slug":"sandbox-closed-area-build","title":"A closed area for lym: bwrap sandbox in stages, off by default","kind":"note","types":["feature"],"areas":["reed","shuttle","claude","fabric","creel","dobby","batten","deploy","config","ide"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Features","waits_on":[],"brief":"Run the lym process tree in a bwrap area with a host proxy and broker, built in three stages with a stop; to be split if it goes ahead","body":"## Seen (operator, 2026-10-30)\n\nEvery agent lym starts runs with `--dangerously-skip-permissions`, which many people do not accept, and a repository that states it supports safe sandboxing is worth more than one that does not.\nThe area matters most on the work machine OSL-1046 (operator, 2026-11-22).\nOn OSL-1046 sudo is tightly limited and does not cover AppArmor, so the profile `bwrap` needs on Ubuntu 24.04 cannot be loaded there; a `bwrap` area, and podman or Claude Code's `/sandbox`, which need the same user namespaces, are blocked on that machine until IT loads it.\nLandlock needs no privilege, but the study `docs/research/platform/landlock-area-2026-11-23.md` (landed) found it does not close the two exposures below on kernel ABI 8 (Neve's measured; OSL-1046's by kernel version): a restricted child still reached the user bus and another hub's tmux socket, and `systemd-run --user` escapes the domain. On ABI 8 a Landlock area alone is a guardrail, not a barrier.\nIt recommends PreToolUse rows in `internal/shellguard` for the bus commands and other hubs' tmux servers, and re-running its probe on each new kernel.\nThe later study `docs/research/platform/sandbox-without-new-kernel-2026-11-23.md` (landed) finds that a seccomp filter handing every `connect()` to a supervisor in lym, stacked over Landlock ABI 8, closes both exposures with no root and no user namespace (measured on Neve; unmeasured on OSL-1046).\nIt recommends the shellguard rows now, that area when a barrier is wanted, after its probe has run on OSL-1046, and an AppArmor profile per hub as the fallback.\nOf the shellguard rows, one is partly built: run sessions are refused raw `tmux` and `script`, the sampler excepted (`33cbce982`), which a script file or `eval` gets past; no row for the bus commands came with it.\nThe operator uses lym alone, on Linux (OSL-1046: Ubuntu 24.04; Neve: Kubuntu on Ubuntu 26.04, Plasma 6), and has no interest in other users or in Windows.\nThe research is `docs/research/platform/sandboxing-options-2026-10-30.md`: it weighs `bwrap`, Landlock, a dedicated Linux user, systemd sandboxing, podman and Claude Code's own `/sandbox`, and the packages and cards each would change.\n\nTwo exposures hold today with no area: an agent can reach `/run/user/\u003cuid\u003e/bus` and through it `systemd-run --user`, which starts any process outside lym's tree, and it can type into other hubs' tmux panes through their sockets in `/tmp/tmux-\u003cuid\u003e`, the Models hub's among them.\n\n## Idea\n\nRun the whole lym process tree inside a `bwrap` area launched by lym itself, with the agents free inside it and a short, controlled list of exits.\nIt is an option the operator turns on, never a requirement: with it off, everything works as it does today, and with it on, nothing starts when the sandbox cannot.\nThe operator thinks the full design is complicated and error-prone, and doubts it will work without large problems, so it is built in stages with a stop between them.\nThis is one large note and is split into tasks, or a project, only when the operator decides it goes ahead.\n\n### Before the area: two cheap steps\n\n- Start a hub's tmux server from a Konsole window, not a VS Code terminal: the server inherits the `vscode` AppArmor profile, which grants user namespaces to every agent of the hub.\n- A line in `README.md` that every agent lym starts runs with bypass permissions, and that lym belongs on a machine the operator is willing to hand to its agents.\n\n### Stage 1: the filesystem area only\n\n`bwrap` with `--unshare-all --die-with-parent --clearenv` around reed's tmux server and everything under it, started by a new `lym area start` and a supervisor inside.\n\n- The hub is bound read-write at its own path, since the tmux server name, the creel socket name, git's worktree links and Claude Code's transcript directory all derive from absolute paths; `/usr`, `/etc` and `/opt` are read-only, `$HOME` a tmpfs with read-only binds of the Go toolchain, the Claude Code binary and the installed `lym`.\n- The area has its own Claude config directory and Go caches bound at the usual paths, with the host's plugin cache read-only on top, so the host's `settings.json` hooks never run in it.\n- One host directory holds the tmux socket directory, so `lym reed attach` from outside drives the server inside; tried on OSL-1046 with a tmux server inside `bwrap`.\n- `.git/hooks` is read-only; `.git/config` was to stay writable for `branch.\u003cb\u003e.lymLocal`, but `fabric-remote-async` removed that mark and `internal/gitrepo/branchmark.go`, so the reason is gone; whether git itself or another lym writer still needs `.git/config` writable is unchecked.\n- Network stays open and there is no broker, so the landing deploy, `lym reed window`, `lym ide` and `lym hibernate request` do not work from inside; the operator does them by hand, from outside.\n- A config module `area` with `required`, off by default; `lym area start` refuses on any failure and never falls back to an unsandboxed start.\n- Ubuntu 24.04 needs a one-time AppArmor profile for `/usr/bin/bwrap`, loaded by root; the report names the profile, and Ubuntu 25.04 and later ship it, which is not verified on Neve.\n\nThis closes the two exposures above and shows early whether tmux, creel and dobby run well inside an area.\n\n### Stop: the operator decides whether stage 1 works well enough to go on.\n\n### Stage 2: egress through a host proxy\n\nNo network inside the area; a host-side proxy, reached through a bound socket, allows CONNECT to `api.anthropic.com`, `claude.ai`, `platform.claude.com`, `proxy.golang.org`, `sum.golang.org`, `github.com` and `api.github.com`, with a loopback relay inside the area.\nTelemetry and the auto-updater are off by environment.\nThe GitHub token handed to the area is fine-grained and limited to the hub's repositories; the operator's `gh` login never enters.\nWebFetch and the `prowler` skill reach only these hosts.\n\n### Stage 3: a broker for the remaining exits\n\nA host-side process serving a fixed set of verbs and never a command line from the area: the landing deploy (it fetches `origin/main` into its own clone, checks the commit is the tip, builds with the host's Go and installs), `lym reed window` opening Konsole, and `lym hibernate request`.\n`lym ide` is refused, since VS Code on the host would run git over repositories the area writes; the host runs no git over the area's repositories at all.\n\n## What is not claimed\n\nIsolation from a kernel exploit, which needs a virtual machine; that data in the area cannot leave through an allowed host; and that a landed change of lym itself cannot run on the host once deployed, which matters on this hub because it develops lym.\nLandlock alone controls no pathname socket until the kernel does (the Landlock study lists the five tasks that area takes; the later study puts Landlock ABI 8 under its seccomp supervisor instead), and Claude Code deny rules in shuttle's settings are a cheap second line inside the area; the dedicated user, podman, systemd sandboxing and Claude Code's `/sandbox` inside lym runs are left out.\nThe report estimates the whole at five tasks and eighteen cards.\n\n## Related\n\nCross-hub creel mail (`b30cccce2`): the inbox folder sits outside an area, so reading and writing it goes through the broker.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-10-30T09:39:39Z","revised":"2026-11-26T08:10:21Z","design_file":"design-sandbox-closed-area-build.md","decision_file":"","revision":"90b3cc2fca89b8f96c9799449305ce12ad6c8c8f06b1642d6b638ee804ea27e0"},{"handle":"F17","slug":"orchestrator-survey-ideas","title":"Remaining leads from the survey of comparable agent orchestrators (Oct 2026)","kind":"note","types":["feature"],"areas":["burler","shed","orch","quarry","report"],"status":"","project":"P2","section":"Features","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Leads borrowed from comparable open-source agent orchestrators, surveyed in October 2026 (first imported from issue #671).\nNothing here is a commitment; these are leads to triage.\nThe orch checked each against the code on 2026-11-17: ideas lym has built or that proved moot are dropped,\npartly built ones name only what is still open, and the eight most worth doing are notes of their own in this project.\nEach bullet names its source project and the lym module it would touch.\n\n## Plan validation and concurrency (quarry, Plan-Write, board)\n\n- **Typed scope operations on cards** (foremerge): each scope carries an additive or breaking operation,\n  and deterministic rules flag colliding pairs even when files do not overlap.\n  Worth comparing against board labels and the alignment gate.\n- **Symbol leases between concurrent tasks** (Fusion, ORCH): expiring leases on symbol keys serialize overlapping tasks,\n  with a deterministic tie-break; lym keeps overlapping tasks apart by board labels.\n- **Commit-time reservation guard** (ntm): a pre-commit hook refuses a commit touching what another task holds; needs leases first.\n- **Criterion amendment ledger** (oh-my-claudecode): a card's acceptance text changes only through a recorded amendment in weft.\n\n## Review (burler, Bouncer)\n\n- **Quorum of differently-angled judges** (ccswarm): lym fans out lenses but has no quorum vote for high-risk segments.\n  Since 2026-11-19 a run's parent orch can be asked to see and veto each Code-Review round and each drift judge ruling\n  (`code_review_ask_parent`, `judge_ask_parent`); that is one extra judge, not a quorum.\n- **Risk tier by changed paths** (yylo): globs over sensitive paths choose the review depth and the gate scope.\n\n## Engine, recipes and governance (shed, tender, orch, board)\n\n- **Cross-run quarantine** (Bernstein): a task that fails three times across runs is held, unless the failures were the host's.\n- **Fork a run from a step with a tree snapshot** (Bernstein): `shed goto` moves the cursor but does not restore the warp tree.\n- **Warm retry guarded by a workspace hash** (Bernstein): resume the native session only when the tree is unchanged.\n  Partly met since `dfade5f9a`: a run agent whose session a crash, reboot or killed pane ended resumes it (engines with a `SessionResumer`, so not Codex);\n  no tree hash guards that resume, and a retry after a timeout or a finished run still starts fresh.\n- **Evidence reuse by input identity** (yylo): verify is skipped on the same tree; a changed review prompt could rerun only the review.\n- **Guidance at the next step boundary** (Conductor): a queue of operator guidance for the next producer's prompt.\n  Partly met since 2026-11-19: `notify_parent_on` in `loom.yaml` mails the run's parent when a named row is reached,\n  so the parent can brief that row's agent as it starts; there is still no queue of guidance lym itself folds into the prompt.\n- **Batched operator questions with recorded assumptions** (Spec Kit `/clarify`, Conductor): Discussion-Write asks at most a few ranked questions,\n  each with a recommended answer, and records unanswered ones as assumptions.\n  Since 2026-11-19 a run's autonomous agents put their questions to the run's parent orch, not to the operator in their pane.\n  Discussion asks in its pane only when `discussion_interactive` is `true`, which is off by default and forced off by autonomy mode (`30ed1ee58`),\n  so this lead concerns an interactive Discussion only.\n- **Rate-limit wait**: the orch watcher wakes the orch after a usage reset; tender itself schedules no resume.\n- **Standing goals and scheduled routines** (ORCH, CAO, Fusion): periodic triage, dependency bumps or `selfreport` sweeps.\n\n## Agent sessions (reed, shuttle)\n\n- **Waiting-for-input signal** (agent-of-empires): shuttle has an awaiting-input state; screen heuristics still decide readiness and startup.\n- **Reference status patterns and a paste pitfall** (CAO): compare its regexes and its tmux bracketed-paste note with shuttle's readiness and creel inject.\n- **Context forecasting** (ntm): the orch cycles its context; Webster's Master and the loom driver do not, and nothing forecasts.\n- **Staggered spawns** (ntm): space Webster forks and batten fan-out by learned rate limits.\n\n## Observability (report, weft)\n\n- **Hash-chained run journal** (Bernstein): a hash chain over producer outputs makes an archived run self-verifying.\n- **Unverified-completion rate** (Bernstein): a `lym report` view of completions with no test or gate behind them.\n- **Commit-to-record links** (AgentNote): weft links to warp by a `Warp-SHA:` trailer; a note or trailer on each warp commit could link back.\n- **Run diff** (ccswarm): `lym report` comparing two archived runs' timelines and token use.\n- **OpenTelemetry spans per producer** (Conductor).\n\n## Operator attention (orch, dobby, reed)\n\n- **Attention feed with acknowledgement states** (ntm): dobby's wait reminders have no seen, snoozed or acknowledged states.\n\n## Discussion review (Discussion-Write, stencils, pattern)\n\n- **Requirements-quality checklist** (Spec Kit `/checklist`): items with IDs the Discussion-review judge answers one by one.\n- **Fixed analysis categories** (Kiro): inconsistency, ambiguity, conflicts, unstated assumptions and missing edge cases as named lenses.\n- **PATTERN rules handed to reviewers** (Spec Kit constitution): its rules as numbered MUSTs in plan and code review prompts.\n- **Glob-scoped steering** (Kiro): stencils pull per-area guidance chosen by the paths of a plan's glyphs.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-10-16T06:52:07Z","revised":"2026-11-26T08:01:36Z","design_file":"design-orchestrator-survey-ideas.md","decision_file":"","revision":"5943695b095748396e00d09d15155719e05150e135b3d238308638c01ea7242a"},{"handle":"R1","slug":"hardener-works-study","title":"Study whether the hardener finds and fixes real faults","kind":"note","types":["research"],"areas":["burler","fabric"],"status":"","project":"P4","section":"Research","waits_on":[],"brief":"Judge the two fabric hardener campaigns from their records: real findings, sound fixes, focus kept, cost","body":"## Goal\n\nFind out whether the hardener recipe does what it is for: drive the modules its focus names, find real faults, fix them with tests that hold, and converge.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- The first fabric campaign, `harden-fabricengine`, landed as `37649c772` after three rounds.\n- The second, `harden-fabric-single-repo-verbs`, the one-repository fabric campaign with the fabric verbs in its sandbox, has landed as `2722a66ef`, so both campaigns are judged.\n- The second campaign ran with the model rotation the template then set (opus, fable, sonnet).\n  Hardener rounds now default to `sonnet[high]` alone, and a rotation is set per run through `hardener.reviewer` and `hardener.fixer` (`a66d41345`), so judge the rotation's cost and effect against that change.\n- The five faults the second campaign found but could not raise were the task `harden-fabric-undriven-cases`, which has landed as `540e8c9b5`.\n  Judge that fix as the campaign's follow-through.\n- The cases round 3 left undriven (a discard or remove killed mid-operation, a fabric step that fails with no kill, and `adopt --archives`) were then driven by hand on a one-repository hub in the task `single-repo-undriven-cases`, landed as `dce6b8617` with the report `docs/research/single-repo-undriven-cases.md`.\n  It found one fault the campaign's rounds had not: the killed run's `remove` record names `lym fabric remove \u003cslug\u003e --remote`, which answers `pair not found` once the pair is gone (the task `remove-record-rerun-pair-gone`).\n  Judge it as a miss: could the campaign have reached it? The keep-local tag push was not among the cases it drove, and a real `adopt --archives` takeover needs a second machine, so the tags were pushed by hand.\n- Each campaign's round files are under its records worktree at `_lym/reviews/harden/`: `round-\u003cn\u003e-focus.md`, `round-\u003cn\u003e-review.md`, `round-\u003cn\u003e-acceptance.md`, `round-\u003cn\u003e-usage.yaml` and the snapshots, beside its decision record and the landed commits.\n\n## Questions\n\n- **Findings**: for each finding, is it a real fault, a false positive or a matter of taste? Do its repro steps show it?\n- **Fixes**: does each fix cure its finding? Does its regression test fail with the fix broken, as the fix rules demand? Was the package doc updated?\n- **Focus**: did each round stay inside the campaign's focus? Did the focus files steer later rounds to what was left unverified?\n- **Convergence**: did the Bouncer end the campaign on a sound verdict? Were deferred findings filed as open risks and on the board?\n- **Misses**: which faults in the same code turned up after the campaign landed, outside it (such as the killed-seed way forward in `0e4782373`, the fetch prune in `f660ac101` and the remove record's way forward in `remove-record-rerun-pair-gone`), and could the campaign have reached them?\n- **Cost**: tokens and wall time per round, from the usage files and the run's records.\n- **Seen in the second campaign**: round 2's reviewer recorded smaller faults (O1 to O6) only as prose, since an incremental round admits only BLOCKING findings on changed hunks.\n  Round 3's reviewer (sonnet, medium, 4 min) approved with half its focus undriven: the killed discard, the keep-local tag push, a failed step with no kill, and `adopt --archives` over real owner tags.\n  A settled harden segment cannot be reopened (`lym loom goto` refuses), so the orch had no way to order another round. Judge each of these, and whether the remaining focus should block approval.\n  Round 4 again raised what it found outside the incremental scope only as prose; those are the O1 to O5 that `harden-fabric-undriven-cases` fixed.\n  A suspected cause is the stencils, not the model: `contracts/stencils/harden/harden-rubric-review.md` asks for OK, WARN or FAIL on every focus entry,\n  while `contracts/stencils/burler/burler-review-format.md` lets an incremental round raise only BLOCKING findings on changed hunks, so such a round can only approve. Judge that conflict.\n  Since `8cf7f4775`, a later hardener round cannot approve with its focus undriven:\n  the reviewer raises a `decision`-class, BLOCKING, `origin: focus` finding for each focus entry it left undriven or marked WARN,\n  `burlerengine.CheckReviewRound` exempts it from the changed-file rule, and the arbiter and then the parent rule on it.\n  That answers whether the remaining focus should block approval going forward; judge the campaign's rounds as they ran, and whether the new finding is enough for the rubric conflict.\n\n## Bounds\n\n- Mostly a study of records and commits.\n- At most three findings are reproduced, on a sandbox hub against the commit before their fix, one try each; no other trial runs.\n- The report ends with a verdict and a short list of concrete changes to the hardener recipe or its stencils, each a candidate note.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-22T17:52:12Z","revised":"2026-11-23T18:23:43Z","design_file":"design-hardener-works-study.md","decision_file":"","revision":"225b7dfc55925192b40881b0323a537ee5d1950b1cb83d70462fc744986ccfdd"},{"handle":"R3","slug":"cortex-cost-measure","title":"Measure what Cortex XDR costs the test suites on OSL-1046","kind":"note","types":["research"],"areas":["gate","fabric"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Research","waits_on":[],"brief":"The 242 s fabricengine tier 1 against 37 to 41 s elsewhere is a machine difference no report has split","body":"## Seen (operator, 2026-11-09)\n\nCortex XDR on OSL-1046 holds each new binary for about 10 s and scans each file a test creates, and fabricengine tests create many.\nThe shard report (`docs/research/speed/fabricengine-shards-2026-11-09/report.md`) put the 242 s one-process wall down to load, but the 2026-11-08 figure of 37 to 41 s was measured on another machine, so the gap is machine and Cortex, not load.\nThe package used 1.23 cores of 16 and `pmd` and `piud` were busy in nearly every sample.\n\n## Idea\n\nSeparate the Cortex cost from the rest: the time a freshly built test binary waits before it runs, the time file creation takes with and without a scanned folder, and the fabricengine tier 1 wall with the repository and temp folders excluded from the scan, if the operator can have that changed.\nThe result says whether to cut tests, cut file and binary creation, or ask for an exclusion.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-09T12:09:36Z","revised":"2026-11-09T12:09:36Z","design_file":"design-cortex-cost-measure.md","decision_file":"","revision":"1c95fc4fc30f94ef252b9aa86d90d381a01f349a7ff43a9218f67bc7f0e647dd"},{"handle":"R4","slug":"antigravity-engine-spike","title":"Antigravity CLI engine spike","kind":"note","types":["research"],"areas":["shuttle"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Research","waits_on":[],"brief":"Hands-on spike of agy in a tmux pane: screens, hooks, transcript, login","body":"## Seen (orch, 2026-11-17)\n\nThe desk study `docs/research/gemini-antigravity-engine-study-2026-11-17.md` (`246c9146`) judged Antigravity CLI (`agy`) the better Google engine target.\nIts terms aim at third-party services reselling access, not at the operator driving the CLI with a tool they wrote, as with Codex and Claude (operator, 2026-11-17).\nSeveral of its readings are undocumented and can only be learnt hands-on.\n\n## Idea\n\nA hands-on spike like Codex's of 2026-10-24, run as darn docs, that installs `agy` and runs it in a tmux pane on Neve.\nIt answers the study's open points:\n\n- the busy, idle, trust and interrupt screens, and bracketed paste plus Enter through `paste-buffer -p`;\n- whether the keyring login works in a lym pane;\n- the hook payloads for SessionStart, PreToolUse, PostToolUse, PostInvocation and Stop (`continue`, `fullyIdle`);\n- the transcript's on-disk layout and token fields;\n- whether a conversation id can be chosen up front, and whether the `self` subagent inherits the caller's history.\n\nCap: one machine, one model, one short session per point, no lym code changed.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-17T18:54:35Z","revised":"2026-11-17T18:54:35Z","design_file":"design-antigravity-engine-spike.md","decision_file":"","revision":"050ddc1b61f6b055aa9d9b6c628533fe44d2d55b1714f47bce2bcd427506c7b5"}]};
